Q1 #### explain how the equals() and hashcode() methods of the object class are related to each other. give an example of what could go wrong if this relationship is not correctly observed

In Java, the relationship between equals() and hashCode() is governed by a strict contract. If you override one, you must override the other. Their partnership is the foundation for how Java handles objects in hash-based collections like HashMap, HashSet, and Hashtable.

The Fundamental Contract
The relationship can be summarized by one golden rule: If two objects are equal according to the equals(Object) method, then calling the hashCode() method on each of the two objects must produce the same integer result.

Equal objects ⟹ Same Hash Code: This is mandatory.

Same Hash Code ⟹ Equal objects: This is not required. Two different objects can have the same hash code (this is called a collision).

How They Work Together in a Collection
When you add an object to a HashSet, Java doesn't immediately run equals() on every item already in the set (which would be slow). Instead:

It calls hashCode() to find the "bucket" where the object should live.

If the bucket is empty, it stays there.

If there are already objects in that bucket (a collision), it then uses equals() to check if the new object is actually the same as the existing ones.

What Goes Wrong: The "Disappearing Object" Problem
If you override equals() but forget hashCode(), you break the contract. Two objects might be "equal" logically, but they will end up with different hash codes and be placed in different buckets.

The Scenario
Imagine a Student class where two objects are considered equal if they have the same id.

Java
public class Student {
    private int id;

    public Student(int id) { this.id = id; }

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object o) {
        if (this == o) return true;
        if (!(o instanceof Student)) return false;
        return id == ((Student) o).id;
    }
    
    // MISSING: hashCode() override
}
The Failure
If you use this class in a HashSet, the collection will behave unpredictably:

Java
HashSet<Student> set = new HashSet<>();
Student s1 = new Student(101);
Student s2 = new Student(101);

set.add(s1);

System.out.println(set.contains(s2)); // Returns FALSE
Why did it fail?
Even though s1.equals(s2) is true, they have different default hash codes (based on their memory addresses).

When you add(s1), it goes into Bucket A.

When you call contains(s2), Java looks in Bucket B because s2 has a different hash code.

Java never even triggers the equals() method because it's looking in the wrong part of the map.

Result: You end up with "duplicate" logical objects in your Set, or you find yourself unable to retrieve an object you just stored.

Best Practice
Always use your IDE's generator or a utility like Objects.hash() to ensure both methods use the same fields:

Java
@Override
public int hashCode() {
    return Objects.hash(id);
}
Q2 ######### explain dependency injection. how does it manage complexity of software

What is Dependency Injection?
Dependency Injection (DI) is a design pattern where an object’s dependencies (the other objects it needs to function) are "injected" into it from the outside, rather than the object creating them itself.

Think of it like a restaurant:

Without DI: A Chef has to leave the kitchen, go to the farm, harvest vegetables, and then cook. The Chef is responsible for "creating" their own ingredients.

With DI: A Supplier delivers the fresh vegetables directly to the kitchen. The Chef just focuses on cooking. The "dependency" (the vegetables) is injected into the Chef's workflow.

How it Manages Software Complexity
DI is one of the most effective tools for managing complexity because it enforces Decoupling and the Single Responsibility Principle. Here is how it helps:

1. Easier Testing (Mocking)
Complexity often makes software hard to test. If a BillingService creates its own DatabaseConnection, you can’t test the billing logic without a real database running.

With DI: You can inject a "Mock" database during testing. This allows you to test logic in isolation without setting up heavy infrastructure.

2. Swap Implementations Without Changing Code
If your application uses an EmailService to send notifications, and you decide to switch from SendGrid to Mailchimp, DI makes this trivial.

Without DI: You would have to find every place you wrote new SendGridService() and change it.

With DI: You change the configuration in one central place (the "Container" or "Injector"), and the rest of the system automatically receives the new service.

3. Clearer Object Lifecycles
In complex systems, managing when an object is created and destroyed is a headache. DI frameworks (like Spring or Dagger) handle the "Object Graph" for you. They decide if an object should be a Singleton (one instance for the whole app) or Transient (a new instance every time it's needed).

4. Reduced "Boilerplate" Code
DI removes the messy setup code from your business logic. Classes become cleaner because they only contain the code necessary for their specific job, while the "wiring" of the system is handled elsewhere.

Code Example: The Transition
The "Hard-Wired" Way (High Complexity)
This class is "tightly coupled" to the Engine. If you want a ElectricEngine, you have to rewrite the Car class.

Java
public class Car {
    private Engine engine;

    public Car() {
        this.engine = new GasEngine(); // Hard-coded dependency
    }
}
The DI Way (Low Complexity)
The Car doesn't care what kind of engine it gets, as long as it fits the Engine interface.

Java
public class Car {
    private Engine engine;

    // The dependency is "injected" via the constructor
    public Car(Engine engine) {
        this.engine = engine;
    }
}
By shifting the responsibility of "finding dependencies" away from the components themselves, you create a system that is modular, flexible, and significantly easier to maintain as it grows.

